      "headline": "Commissioning of the Septuagint",
      "text": "According to the Letter of Aristeas, the Septuagint was commissioned by Ptolemy II Philadelphus, who reigned from 283 to 246 BCE, to translate the Hebrew Bible into Greek. This was intended to be part of the library of Alexandria.",
      "date": "-0283"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Translation of the Torah",
      "text": "The translation of the Torah, the first five books of the Hebrew Bible, is traditionally believed to have been completed by 72 translators in approximately 70 days. This event is dated around the early to mid-3rd century BCE.",
      "date": "-0250"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Septuagint",
      "text": "The translation of the entire Hebrew Bible into Greek, known as the Septuagint, was completed over the course of the 3rd to 2nd centuries BCE. The exact date of completion is not known, but it is generally considered to have been finished by the end of the 2nd century BCE.",
      "date": "-0100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Early Christianity",
      "text": "The Septuagint became widely used in the early Christian Church as the Old Testament. It was the version of the Old Testament that many early Christians, including the authors of the New Testament, would have been familiar with.",
      "date": "0001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on Christian Theology",
      "text": "The Septuagint played a significant role in the development of Christian theology and biblical interpretation, as it was the primary source of Old Testament quotations in the New Testament.",
      "date": "0100"
    }
  ]
}

