      "headline": "Establishment of Air Cess",
      "text": "In the early 1990s, Bout established Air Cess, an air freight business that would later be linked to arms trafficking.",
      "date": "1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arms Trafficking Operations",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and early 2000s, Bout used his companies to smuggle arms from Eastern Europe to conflict zones in Africa and the Middle East.",
      "date": "1990s-2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UN Report and Nicknames",
      "text": "In 2003, British minister Peter Hain referred to Bout as the 'Merchant of Death' and 'Sanctions Buster' in a report to the United Nations, highlighting his extensive arms trafficking operations.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest in Thailand",
      "text": "On March 6, 2008, Viktor Bout was arrested in Bangkok, Thailand, following a sting operation led by the U.S. Drug Enforcement Administration.",
      "date": "2008-03-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extradition to the United States",
      "text": "Bout was extradited to the United States on November 16, 2010, to face charges of conspiring to provide material support to a terrorist organization.",
      "date": "2010-11-16"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conviction and Sentencing",
      "text": "On November 2, 2011, Bout was convicted by a U.S. federal court of conspiring to kill U.S. citizens and officials, deliver anti-aircraft missiles, and provide aid to a terrorist organization. He was sentenced to 25 years in prison on April 5, 2012.",
      "date": "2012-04-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Prisoner Swap and Release",
      "text": "On December 8, 2022, Viktor Bout was released from U.S. custody as part of a prisoner swap for American basketball player Brittney Griner.",
