      "headline": "Discovery of Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR)",
      "text": "In 1946, Felix Bloch and Edward Purcell independently discovered nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), a phenomenon that would later become the basis for MRI technology. They were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1952 for this discovery.",
      "date": "1946"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First MRI Image",
      "text": "In 1973, Paul Lauterbur published the first MRI image. He developed a technique to create two-dimensional images using magnetic field gradients, which was a significant breakthrough in the development of MRI technology.",
      "date": "1973"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of MRI by Peter Mansfield",
      "text": "In the mid-1970s, Peter Mansfield further developed MRI technology by introducing the echo-planar imaging technique, which allowed for faster image acquisition. This advancement was crucial for the practical use of MRI in medical diagnostics.",
      "date": "1970s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Human MRI Scan",
      "text": "In 1977, the first MRI scan of a human was performed. This marked a significant milestone in the application of MRI technology for medical purposes.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine",
      "text": "In 2003, Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their discoveries concerning magnetic resonance imaging.",
      "date": "2003"
    }
  ]
}

