      "headline": "Discovery by Europeans",
      "text": "The Galápagos Islands were discovered by Europeans in 1535 when the Spanish bishop of Panama, Fray Tomás de Berlanga, accidentally sailed to the islands while en route to Peru.",
      "date": "1535"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Map of the Islands",
      "text": "The first map of the Galápagos Islands was created by the pirate Ambrose Cowley in 1684. He named the individual islands after some of his fellow pirates or after British royalty and nobility.",
      "date": "1684"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Charles Darwin's Visit",
      "text": "Charles Darwin visited the Galápagos Islands in 1835 during his voyage on the HMS Beagle. His observations of the islands' unique species contributed to the development of his theory of evolution by natural selection.",
      "date": "1835"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annexation by Ecuador",
      "text": "The Galápagos Islands were annexed by Ecuador on February 12, 1832. The islands were initially used as a penal colony.",
      "date": "1832-02-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Galápagos National Park",
      "text": "The Galápagos National Park was established in 1959, protecting 97% of the land area of the islands.",
      "date": "1959"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UNESCO World Heritage Site",
      "text": "The Galápagos Islands were designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1978 due to their unique biodiversity and significance to the study of evolution.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of the Galápagos Marine Reserve",
      "text": "The Galápagos Marine Reserve was established in 1998, extending protection to the waters surrounding the islands.",
      "date": "1998"
    }
  ]
}

