      "text": "Opium, the natural source of opioids, has been used since ancient times. The earliest reference to opium cultivation and use is from the Sumerians in Mesopotamia around 3400 BCE.",
      "date": "-3400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isolation of Morphine",
      "text": "In 1804, Friedrich Sertürner, a German pharmacist, isolated morphine from opium, marking the first time an active ingredient was extracted from a plant.",
      "date": "1804"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Heroin",
      "text": "In 1898, Bayer, a German pharmaceutical company, introduced heroin (diacetylmorphine) as a cough suppressant and non-addictive substitute for morphine.",
      "date": "1898"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Controlled Substances Act",
      "text": "In 1970, the United States passed the Controlled Substances Act, which classified drugs into schedules and regulated opioids under strict guidelines.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of OxyContin",
      "text": "In 1996, Purdue Pharma introduced OxyContin, a time-released formulation of oxycodone, which became widely prescribed for pain management.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Opioid Crisis Declared a Public Health Emergency",
      "text": "In October 2017, the opioid crisis was declared a public health emergency in the United States due to the rising number of opioid-related overdoses and deaths.",
      "date": "2017-10"
    }
  ]
}

