    {
      "headline": "Cretaceous–Paleogene Extinction Event",
      "text": "Approximately 66 million years ago, the Cretaceous–Paleogene (K–Pg) extinction event occurred, leading to the mass extinction of three-quarters of plant and animal species on Earth. This event marked the end of the Cretaceous period and the Mesozoic era, and the beginning of the Cenozoic era.",
      "date": "-66000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extinction of Non-Avian Dinosaurs",
      "text": "During the K–Pg extinction event, all non-avian dinosaurs became extinct. This marked a significant shift in the Earth's biological diversity.",
      "date": "-66000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Survival of Certain Species",
      "text": "Most tetrapods weighing more than 25 kg (55 lb) became extinct, except for some ectothermic species such as sea turtles and crocodilians, which survived the K–Pg extinction event.",
      "date": "-66000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "K–Pg Boundary Formation",
      "text": "The K–Pg event is marked in the geologic record by a thin layer of sediment known as the K–Pg boundary, which is found worldwide in marine and terrestrial rocks. This boundary clay contains unusually high levels of iridium, a metal more common in asteroids than in the Earth's crust.",
      "date": "-66000000"
    }
  ]
}

