      "headline": "First Observation of Electroluminescence",
      "text": "In 1907, British experimenter H. J. Round of Marconi Labs was the first to report electroluminescence from a solid-state diode made of silicon carbide.",
      "date": "1907"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of the First Infrared LED",
      "text": "In 1961, James R. Biard and Gary Pittman, working at Texas Instruments, created the first infrared LED.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Practical Visible-Spectrum LED",
      "text": "In 1962, Nick Holonyak Jr., while working at General Electric, developed the first practical visible-spectrum (red) LED.",
      "date": "1962"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Yellow LEDs",
      "text": "In 1972, M. George Craford, a former graduate student of Holonyak, invented the first yellow LED and improved the brightness of red and red-orange LEDs by a factor of ten.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of High-Brightness Blue LED",
      "text": "In 1994, Shuji Nakamura of Nichia Corporation developed the first high-brightness blue LED using gallium nitride.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Prize in Physics for Blue LED",
      "text": "In 2014, Isamu Akasaki, Hiroshi Amano, and Shuji Nakamura were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics for the invention of efficient blue LEDs, which enabled bright and energy-saving white light sources.",
      "date": "2014"
    }
  ]
}

