      "headline": "Reichstag Fire",
      "text": "On 27 February 1933, the Reichstag building, home of the German parliament in Berlin, was set on fire. The arson attack occurred precisely four weeks after Adolf Hitler was sworn in as Chancellor of Germany.",
      "date": "1933-02-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest of Marinus van der Lubbe",
      "text": "Marinus van der Lubbe, a Dutch council communist, was arrested at the scene of the Reichstag fire and later charged with arson.",
      "date": "1933-02-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reichstag Fire Decree Issued",
      "text": "On 28 February 1933, President Paul von Hindenburg, under the influence of the Nazis, issued the Reichstag Fire Decree. This decree suspended civil liberties and allowed for the arrest of political opponents, particularly Communists.",
      "date": "1933-02-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on German Politics",
      "text": "The Reichstag fire was used by the Nazis as a pretext to claim that Communists were plotting against the German government. This event was pivotal in the establishment of Nazi Germany as it allowed for a 'ruthless confrontation' with the Communists.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reichstag Fire Trial",
      "text": "The trial for the Reichstag fire began in September 1933. Marinus van der Lubbe was the main defendant, and the trial was used by the Nazis to further their political agenda against Communists.",
      "date": "1933-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Execution of Marinus van der Lubbe",
      "text": "Marinus van der Lubbe was found guilty of arson and executed by guillotine on 10 January 1934.",
      "date": "1934-01-10"
    }
  ]
}

