      "text": "Guru Nanak was born on April 15, 1469, in the village of Rai Bhoi Ki Talwandi, now known as Nankana Sahib, in present-day Pakistan.",
      "date": "1469-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early Education and Spiritual Inclination",
      "text": "From a young age, Guru Nanak showed a keen interest in spirituality and religious discussions. He was educated in both Hindu and Muslim traditions.",
      "date": "1480"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Mata Sulakhni",
      "text": "In 1487, Guru Nanak married Mata Sulakhni, the daughter of Mool Chand and Chando Rani. They had two sons, Sri Chand and Lakhmi Das.",
      "date": "1487"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revelation at the River",
      "text": "In 1496, Guru Nanak had a profound spiritual experience while bathing in the Kali Bein river. He disappeared for three days and emerged with a new spiritual vision, proclaiming, 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim.'",
      "date": "1496"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Udasi (Journey)",
      "text": "Guru Nanak embarked on his first of four major spiritual journeys (Udasis) around 1500, traveling across India and beyond to spread his teachings.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of Kartarpur",
      "text": "In 1522, Guru Nanak founded the town of Kartarpur (now in Pakistan), where he established a commune and continued to preach his message of equality and devotion.",
      "date": "1522"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Compilation of Guru Nanak's Hymns",
      "text": "During his lifetime, Guru Nanak composed numerous hymns that were later compiled into the Guru Granth Sahib, the holy scripture of Sikhism.",
      "date": "1530"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Passing of Guru Nanak",
      "text": "Guru Nanak passed away on September 22, 1539, in Kartarpur. Before his death, he appointed Bhai Lehna as his successor, who became Guru Angad, the second Sikh Guru.",
      "date": "1539-09-22"
    }
  ]
}

