    {
      "headline": "Origin of the Term 'Gerrymandering'",
      "text": "The term 'gerrymandering' originated in 1812 when Massachusetts Governor Elbridge Gerry signed a bill that redistricted the state to benefit his Democratic-Republican Party. The shape of one of the districts was said to resemble a salamander, leading to the term 'Gerry-mander'.",
      "date": "1812"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Use of the Term in Print",
      "text": "The term 'gerrymander' was first used in print in the Boston Gazette on March 26, 1812, in reference to the redistricting plan signed by Governor Elbridge Gerry.",
      "date": "1812-03-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gerrymandering in the United States",
      "text": "Gerrymandering has been a persistent issue in the United States, with various court cases and legislation attempting to address its impact on electoral fairness. The practice has been used by both major political parties to gain electoral advantages.",
      "date": "Various"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Baker v. Carr",
      "text": "In 1962, the U.S. Supreme Court case Baker v. Carr established the principle of 'one person, one vote', which aimed to address the issue of unequal representation due to gerrymandering.",
      "date": "1962"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Shaw v. Reno",
      "text": "In 1993, the U.S. Supreme Court case Shaw v. Reno addressed racial gerrymandering, ruling that redistricting based on race must be held to a standard of strict scrutiny under the equal protection clause.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rucho v. Common Cause",
      "text": "In 2019, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled in Rucho v. Common Cause that federal courts do not have the authority to rule on cases of partisan gerrymandering, leaving the issue to be addressed by state courts and legislatures.",
      "date": "2019"
    }
  ]
}

