      "headline": "Early Horror Films",
      "text": "The horror film genre began in the early 20th century, with the first horror-themed films appearing in the silent film era. Notable early examples include 'Le Manoir du Diable' (1896) by Georges Méliès, often considered the first horror film.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "German Expressionism Influence",
      "text": "During the 1920s, German Expressionist films such as 'The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari' (1920) and 'Nosferatu' (1922) had a significant impact on the horror genre, introducing stylistic elements like distorted sets and shadows.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Universal Monsters Era",
      "text": "The 1930s saw the rise of Universal Pictures' classic monster films, including 'Dracula' (1931), 'Frankenstein' (1931), and 'The Mummy' (1932), establishing iconic horror characters.",
      "date": "1931"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-War Horror",
      "text": "In the 1950s, horror films began to incorporate themes of science fiction, with movies like 'The Thing from Another World' (1951) and 'Invasion of the Body Snatchers' (1956) reflecting Cold War anxieties.",
      "date": "1951"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Psycho and the Slasher Genre",
      "text": "Alfred Hitchcock's 'Psycho' (1960) introduced psychological horror and is credited with laying the groundwork for the slasher genre.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Independent Horror",
      "text": "The 1970s saw a rise in independent horror films, with influential works like 'The Texas Chain Saw Massacre' (1974) and 'Halloween' (1978), which popularized the slasher genre.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Horror in the 1980s",
      "text": "The 1980s were marked by the proliferation of horror franchises, including 'Friday the 13th' (1980) and 'A Nightmare on Elm Street' (1984), which became cultural phenomena.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of Horror in the 1990s",
      "text": "The horror genre experienced a revival in the 1990s with films like 'Scream' (1996), which revitalized the slasher genre with its self-referential style.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Horror Trends",
      "text": "In the 2000s and 2010s, horror films began to explore new themes and subgenres, with movies like 'The Blair Witch Project' (1999) popularizing found footage, and 'Get Out' (2017) addressing social issues.",
      "date": "1999"
    }
  ]
}

