      "text": "The Stranger, a novella by Albert Camus, was first published in 1942. It is one of Camus's most famous works and is considered a classic of existential literature.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plot Overview - Part One",
      "text": "The first part of The Stranger follows Meursault, a French Algerian, who attends his mother's funeral. His emotional detachment and indifference to social norms are highlighted.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Plot Overview - Part Two",
      "text": "In the second part of the novella, Meursault is arrested and tried for the murder of an Arab man. The narrative explores themes of absurdism and the human condition.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Themes and Philosophical Context",
      "text": "The Stranger is often associated with the philosophy of absurdism, which Camus explored in his essay The Myth of Sisyphus, published in the same year, 1942.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
