      "headline": "Battle of Antietam Begins",
      "text": "The Battle of Antietam, also known as the Battle of Sharpsburg, began on September 17, 1862. It was fought between Confederate General Robert E. Lee's Army of Northern Virginia and Union Major General George B. McClellan's Army of the Potomac near Sharpsburg, Maryland, and Antietam Creek.",
      "date": "1862-09-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bloodiest Day in American History",
      "text": "September 17, 1862, marked the bloodiest single day in American history, with a combined tally of 22,727 dead, wounded, or missing soldiers from both the Union and Confederate sides.",
      "date": "1862-09-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Union Tactical Victory",
      "text": "Despite suffering heavier casualties than the Confederates, the Union Army achieved a tactical victory. The battle was a major turning point in the Union's favor, as it halted Lee's invasion of the North.",
      "date": "1862-09-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emancipation Proclamation Issued",
      "text": "Following the Union's strategic advantage gained at the Battle of Antietam, President Abraham Lincoln issued the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation on September 22, 1862, declaring that all slaves in Confederate-held territory would be free as of January 1, 1863.",
      "date": "1862-09-22"
    }
  ]
}

