      "headline": "Proto-Germanic Language",
      "text": "Proto-Germanic, the common ancestor of all Germanic languages, was spoken during the Iron Age in Scandinavia, Northern Germany, and along the North Sea and Baltic coasts. It is believed to have been spoken around 500 BCE.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Germanic Migrations",
      "text": "Around 200 BCE, Germanic tribes began migrating southwards and eastwards, spreading their language and culture across Europe.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tacitus' Germania",
      "text": "In 98 CE, the Roman historian Tacitus wrote 'Germania', a detailed ethnographic work on the Germanic tribes, providing one of the earliest written records of the Germanic peoples.",
      "date": "0098"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gothic Bible Translation",
      "text": "In the 4th century, the Gothic bishop Ulfilas translated the Bible into the Gothic language, marking the first significant literary work in a Germanic language.",
      "date": "0350"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Old English and Old High German",
      "text": "By the 5th century, the Germanic languages had diversified into distinct groups, including Old English in Britain and Old High German in Central Europe.",
      "date": "0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Viking Age and Old Norse",
      "text": "During the Viking Age (8th to 11th centuries), Old Norse was spoken across Scandinavia and in Viking settlements, influencing other languages through Norse expansion.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Middle English Emergence",
      "text": "Following the Norman Conquest of England in 1066, Old English evolved into Middle English, incorporating many Norman French elements.",
      "date": "1066"
    },
    {
      "headline": "High German Consonant Shift",
      "text": "Between the 4th and 9th centuries, the High German consonant shift occurred, distinguishing High German dialects from other West Germanic languages.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Germanic Languages",
      "text": "By the 16th century, the Germanic languages had evolved into their modern forms, including English, German, Dutch, and the Scandinavian languages.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "English as a Global Language",
      "text": "In the 20th and 21st centuries, English became the world's most widely spoken language, with an estimated 2 billion speakers globally.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

