      "headline": "Construction of the Trojan Horse",
      "text": "According to Virgil's Aeneid, after a fruitless 10-year siege of Troy, the Greeks constructed a huge wooden horse at the behest of Odysseus. This event is part of the mythological narrative of the Trojan War.",
      "date": "-0120"  },
    {
      "headline": "The Greeks Pretend to Retreat",
      "text": "The Greeks pretended to sail away, leaving the Trojan Horse behind as a supposed offering to the gods, which was actually a strategic ploy to infiltrate the city of Troy.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Trojans Bring the Horse into the City",
      "text": "The Trojans, believing the war to be over and the horse to be a victory trophy, pulled the wooden horse into the city of Troy.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Greek Soldiers Emerge from the Horse",
      "text": "That night, the Greek force, hidden inside the Trojan Horse, crept out and opened the gates of Troy for the rest of the Greek army, which had returned under the cover of darkness.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction of Troy",
      "text": "The Greeks entered and destroyed the city of Troy, effectively ending the Trojan War. This event is a significant part of Greek mythology and is recounted in various ancient texts.",
      "date": "-0120"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Worship in Sumer",
      "text": "Inanna was originally worshipped in Sumer, where she was revered as the goddess of love, war, and fertility. Her worship dates back to the early dynastic period of Sumer, around 4000 BCE.",
      "date": "-4000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Association with the planet Venus",
      "text": "Inanna was associated with the planet Venus, which was seen as a symbol of her dual nature as both a goddess of love and war. This association likely began during the early dynastic period of Sumer.",
      "date": "-4000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Epic of Gilgamesh",
      "text": "Inanna, known as Ishtar in the Akkadian version, plays a significant role in the Epic of Gilgamesh, which was composed during the Old Babylonian period, around 2100 BCE.",
      "date": "-2100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transformation into Ishtar",
      "text": "Inanna became known as Ishtar in the Akkadian Empire, Babylonia, and Assyria. This transformation occurred as her worship spread throughout Mesopotamia, around 2000 BCE.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Descent of Inanna",
      "text": "The myth 'The Descent of Inanna' describes her journey to the underworld and is one of the earliest known literary works. It was composed during the late third millennium BCE.",
      "date": "-2100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Queen of Heaven",
      "text": "Inanna was often referred to as the 'Queen of Heaven,' a title that emphasized her supreme status among the gods. This title was used throughout her worship in Mesopotamia.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on later cultures",
      "text": "Inanna/Ishtar's influence extended beyond Mesopotamia, impacting later cultures such as the Hittites, Canaanites, and even the Greeks and Romans, who associated her with Aphrodite and Venus.",
      "date": "-1500"
    }
  ]
}

