{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Morphine",
      "text": "Morphine was first isolated between 1803 and 1805 by Friedrich Sertürner, a German pharmacist. It was the first active alkaloid extracted from the opium poppy.",
      "date": "1805"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Morphine",
      "text": "Friedrich Sertürner named the compound 'morphium' after Morpheus, the Greek god of dreams, due to its sleep-inducing properties.",
      "date": "1817"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commercial Production Begins",
      "text": "In 1827, the pharmaceutical company Merck began the commercial production of morphine.",
      "date": "1827"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Hypodermic Needle",
      "text": "The hypodermic needle was invented in 1853, allowing morphine to be administered via injection, which increased its use in medical settings.",
      "date": "1853"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Use in the American Civil War",
      "text": "During the American Civil War (1861-1865), morphine was widely used to treat pain and dysentery among soldiers, leading to many cases of addiction.",
      "date": "1861"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Morphine Addiction",
      "text": "By the late 19th century, the addictive properties of morphine became widely recognized, leading to the term 'soldier's disease' to describe addiction among veterans.",
      "date": "1870"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Long-acting Formulations",
      "text": "Long-acting formulations of morphine, such as MS Contin and Kadian, were developed to provide extended pain relief.",
