      "headline": "Establishment of the Nobel Peace Prize",
      "text": "The Nobel Peace Prize was established by the will of Swedish industrialist, inventor, and armaments manufacturer Alfred Nobel. It was intended to be awarded to individuals or organizations who have done the most or the best work for fraternity between nations, for the abolition or reduction of standing armies, and for the holding and promotion of peace congresses.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Nobel Peace Prize Awarded",
      "text": "The first Nobel Peace Prize was awarded in December 1901. The prize was shared between Henry Dunant, the founder of the International Committee of the Red Cross, and Frédéric Passy, a leading international pacifist of the time.",
      "date": "1901-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Peace Prize Awarded to the International Committee of the Red Cross",
      "text": "The International Committee of the Red Cross was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for the first time in 1917, recognizing its efforts during World War I. The organization would go on to win the prize two more times, in 1944 and 1963.",
      "date": "1917"
    },
    {
