      "headline": "Birth of Theodor Herzl",
      "text": "Theodor Herzl was born on May 2, 1860, in Pest, Kingdom of Hungary, which was part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.",
      "date": "1860-05-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Education and Early Career",
      "text": "Herzl studied law at the University of Vienna and the University of Budapest, graduating in 1884. He initially worked as a lawyer before turning to journalism and literature.",
      "date": "1884"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Der Judenstaat'",
      "text": "In 1896, Herzl published 'Der Judenstaat' (The Jewish State), a pamphlet that outlined his vision for the establishment of a Jewish state as a solution to the Jewish question.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Zionist Congress",
      "text": "Herzl organized the First Zionist Congress, which took place in Basel, Switzerland, from August 29 to August 31, 1897. This congress marked the formal establishment of the Zionist Organization.",
      "date": "1897-08-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Basel Program",
      "text": "During the First Zionist Congress, the Basel Program was adopted, which aimed to establish a 'publicly and legally assured home' for the Jewish people in Palestine.",
      "date": "1897-08-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Meeting with Sultan Abdul Hamid II",
      "text": "In 1901, Herzl met with Sultan Abdul Hamid II of the Ottoman Empire to discuss the possibility of establishing a Jewish homeland in Palestine, although the negotiations were unsuccessful.",
      "date": "1901"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Theodor Herzl",
      "text": "Theodor Herzl died on July 3, 1904, in Edlach, Austria-Hungary, at the age of 44. He was initially buried in Vienna, but his remains were later moved to Mount Herzl in Jerusalem in 1949.",
      "date": "1904-07-03"
    }
  ]
}

