      "text": "In 1919, following World War I, South Tyrol was annexed by Italy from the Austro-Hungarian Empire as part of the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye.",
      "date": "1919"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Italianization Campaign",
      "text": "During the 1920s and 1930s, the Fascist regime in Italy implemented a policy of Italianization in South Tyrol, which included measures such as banning the German language in schools and public offices.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Option Agreement",
      "text": "In 1939, the Option Agreement between Adolf Hitler and Benito Mussolini forced the German-speaking population of South Tyrol to choose between emigration to Nazi Germany or remaining in Italy and undergoing Italianization.",
      "date": "1939"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Paris Agreement",
      "text": "In 1946, the Paris Agreement was signed, granting South Tyrol a degree of autonomy and protection for the German-speaking population.",
      "date": "1946"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Autonomy Statute",
      "text": "In 1972, a new Autonomy Statute was enacted, providing South Tyrol with extensive self-government and recognizing the rights of the German-speaking population.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UN Resolution",
      "text": "In 1992, the United Nations officially recognized the resolution of the South Tyrol dispute between Austria and Italy, acknowledging the successful implementation of the autonomy measures.",
      "date": "1992"
    }
  ]
}

