      "headline": "Introduction of Big O Notation",
      "text": "Big O notation was introduced by German mathematician Paul Bachmann in his 1894 book 'Analytische Zahlentheorie'. The notation was used to describe the limiting behavior of a function when the argument tends towards a particular value or infinity.",
      "date": "1894"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Further Development by Edmund Landau",
      "text": "Edmund Landau further developed and popularized Big O notation in the early 20th century. He used it extensively in his works on number theory and analysis.",
      "date": "1909"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bachmann–Landau Notation",
      "text": "The notation became part of a family of notations known as Bachmann–Landau notation or asymptotic notation, which includes other symbols like Theta (Θ) and Omega (Ω) to describe different types of asymptotic behavior.",
      "date": "Early 20th century"
    }
  ]
}

