      "text": "Babylonia emerged as an Akkadian populated but Amorite-ruled state around 1894 BC.",
      "date": "-1894"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Hammurabi",
      "text": "Hammurabi became the sixth king of the First Babylonian Dynasty, reigning from 1792 BC to 1750 BC. He is best known for his code of laws, the Code of Hammurabi.",
      "date": "-1792"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hammurabi's Code",
      "text": "Around 1754 BC, Hammurabi enacted one of the earliest and most complete written legal codes, known as the Code of Hammurabi.",
      "date": "-1754"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Babylonian Empire",
      "text": "Under Hammurabi, Babylonia became the major power in the region, creating a short-lived empire that succeeded the earlier Akkadian Empire, Third Dynasty of Ur, and Old Assyrian Empire.",
      "date": "-1750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Hammurabi",
      "text": "Hammurabi died in 1750 BC, leading to the rapid decline of the Babylonian Empire.",
      "date": "-1750"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kassite Dynasty",
      "text": "The Kassites took control of Babylonia around 1595 BC, marking the beginning of the Kassite Dynasty which lasted for about 400 years.",
      "date": "-1595"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of Babylon to Hittites",
      "text": "In 1595 BC, Babylon was sacked by the Hittites, leading to a period of instability.",
      "date": "-1595"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of the Neo-Babylonian Empire",
      "text": "The Neo-Babylonian Empire, also known as the Chaldean Empire, rose to prominence in 626 BC under Nabopolassar, marking a resurgence of Babylonian power.",
      "date": "-0626"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Nebuchadnezzar II",
      "text": "Nebuchadnezzar II reigned from 605 BC to 562 BC, during which he expanded the empire and initiated massive building projects, including the Hanging Gardens of Babylon.",
      "date": "-0605"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fall of Babylon to Persia",
      "text": "In 539 BC, Babylon fell to the Persian Empire under Cyrus the Great, marking the end of the Neo-Babylonian Empire.",
      "date": "-0539"
    }
  ]
}

