      "headline": "Emergence of Art Nouveau",
      "text": "Art Nouveau began to emerge as a distinct style in the early 1890s. It was characterized by its use of natural forms, sinuous curves, and modern materials.",
      "date": "1890"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Victor Horta's Hôtel Tassel",
      "text": "In 1893, Victor Horta completed the Hôtel Tassel in Brussels, one of the first buildings to fully embody the Art Nouveau style with its innovative use of iron and glass.",
      "date": "1893"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Art Nouveau Exhibition",
      "text": "The first major exhibition dedicated to Art Nouveau took place in 1895 at the Maison de l'Art Nouveau in Paris, organized by art dealer Siegfried Bing.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Paris Metro Entrances",
      "text": "In 1900, Hector Guimard designed the iconic entrances for the Paris Métro, which became a symbol of Art Nouveau with their organic forms and use of cast iron.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exposition Universelle in Paris",
      "text": "The 1900 Exposition Universelle in Paris showcased Art Nouveau on an international stage, featuring works by artists and architects from around the world.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Art Nouveau",
      "text": "By 1910, Art Nouveau began to decline in popularity, as new styles such as Art Deco and Modernism started to emerge.",
      "date": "1910"
    }
  ]
}

