      "headline": "Composition of the Rigveda",
      "text": "The Rigveda is believed to have been composed between 1500 BCE and 1200 BCE. It is a collection of Vedic Sanskrit hymns and is one of the oldest known texts in any Indo-European language.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Compilation of the Rigveda",
      "text": "The hymns of the Rigveda were compiled into ten books, known as Mandalas, over several centuries. This process is thought to have been completed by around 1200 BCE.",
      "date": "-1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Preservation through Oral Tradition",
      "text": "The Rigveda was preserved through an oral tradition for centuries before being written down. This oral tradition involved precise memorization and recitation techniques.",
      "date": "-1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Śakalya Shakha",
      "text": "The Śakalya Shakha is the only surviving recension of the Rigveda. It is believed to have been developed and preserved around 500 BCE.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Written Manuscripts",
      "text": "The first written manuscripts of the Rigveda are believed to have been created around the 11th century CE, although the exact date is uncertain.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Translation and Study in the West",
      "text": "The Rigveda was first translated into a Western language in the 19th century. Friedrich Max Müller was one of the first scholars to translate and study the text extensively.",
      "date": "1800"
    }
  ]
}

