      "text": "Spironolactone was discovered in the late 1950s as a result of research into steroidal compounds that could act as diuretics and antihypertensives.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval by the FDA",
      "text": "Spironolactone was approved for medical use in the United States by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 1960.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction to Market",
      "text": "Spironolactone was introduced to the market under the brand name Aldactone, among others, and became widely used for its diuretic and antihypertensive properties.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Treating Heart Failure",
      "text": "In the 1980s and 1990s, spironolactone began to be used more extensively in the treatment of heart failure, particularly after studies showed its effectiveness in reducing mortality.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use in Hormone Therapy",
      "text": "Spironolactone started being used as part of hormone therapy for transgender women and nonbinary people to block the effects of testosterone.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Use for Acne and Hirsutism",
      "text": "By the 2000s, spironolactone became a common treatment for acne and excessive hair growth (hirsutism) in women due to its anti-androgen effects.",
      "date": "2000"
