      "headline": "First Publication",
      "text": "The song 'Auld Lang Syne' was first published by Robert Burns in 1788. Burns sent a copy of the original song to the Scots Musical Museum with a note, 'The following song, an old song, of the olden times, and which has never been in print, nor even in manuscript until I took it down from an old man.'",
      "date": "1788"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Melody Association",
      "text": "The melody most commonly associated with 'Auld Lang Syne' is a pentatonic Scots folk melody. It was first published in 1799 in 'Thomson's Select Songs of Scotland'.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New Year's Eve Tradition",
      "text": "The tradition of singing 'Auld Lang Syne' on New Year's Eve became popular in the 19th century. It is often attributed to the Scots, who emigrated around the world and brought the song with them.",
      "date": "1800s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Guy Lombardo's Influence",
      "text": "Guy Lombardo and His Royal Canadians popularized 'Auld Lang Syne' in North America. They performed it on New Year's Eve broadcasts from the late 1920s until 1976, cementing its association with the holiday.",
      "date": "1929"
    }
  ]
}

