      "text": "In 1500, Spanish explorer Vicente Yáñez Pinzón became the first European to discover the mouth of the Amazon River.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Francisco de Orellana's Expedition",
      "text": "In 1541, Spanish explorer Francisco de Orellana led the first known expedition to navigate the entire length of the Amazon River.",
      "date": "1541"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of the Amazon River",
      "text": "The river was named 'Amazon' after Orellana's expedition, during which he reported battles with tribes of warrior women, reminiscent of the Amazons in Greek mythology.",
      "date": "1541"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Scientific Exploration",
      "text": "In 1637-1638, Portuguese explorer Pedro Teixeira led the first scientific exploration of the Amazon River, traveling upstream from its mouth to Quito.",
      "date": "1637"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Charles Marie de La Condamine's Expedition",
      "text": "In 1743, French explorer Charles Marie de La Condamine conducted a scientific expedition to measure the length of the Amazon River.",
      "date": "1743"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Henry Walter Bates' Exploration",
      "text": "In 1848, British naturalist Henry Walter Bates began an 11-year exploration of the Amazon, documenting its biodiversity.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Amazon Rubber Boom",
      "text": "From the late 19th century to the early 20th century, the Amazon region experienced a rubber boom, significantly impacting its economy and environment.",
      "date": "1879"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Amazon's Source",
      "text": "In 1996, an expedition led by Jacek Palkiewicz identified the source of the Amazon River as the Apacheta Creek on Nevado Mismi in the Peruvian Andes.",
      "date": "1996"
    }
  ]
}

