      "headline": "Creation of the Book of Kells",
      "text": "The Book of Kells is believed to have been created around 800 AD. It was produced in a Columban monastery, possibly on the island of Iona, Scotland, or in Ireland. The manuscript is an illuminated Gospel book in Latin, containing the four Gospels of the New Testament.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Relocation to the Abbey of Kells",
      "text": "The Book of Kells was moved to the Abbey of Kells in County Meath, Ireland, likely due to Viking raids on Iona. It remained at the Abbey of Kells for centuries, which is how it got its name.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Survival through Viking Raids",
      "text": "During the 9th century, the Book of Kells survived numerous Viking raids, which were common in the region at the time. The manuscript was kept safe despite the turmoil.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Book of Kells in the 12th Century",
      "text": "By the 12th century, the Book of Kells was still housed at the Abbey of Kells. It was revered as a sacred relic and used in religious ceremonies.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transfer to Trinity College Dublin",
      "text": "In 1661, the Book of Kells was transferred to Trinity College Dublin for safekeeping. It has been housed there ever since, where it is on permanent display.",
      "date": "1661"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conservation and Public Display",
      "text": "The Book of Kells underwent conservation efforts in the 20th century to preserve its intricate illustrations and text. It remains one of the most famous medieval manuscripts and is a major tourist attraction.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

