      "headline": "Origin of Arabic Numerals",
      "text": "Arabic numerals originated in India as part of the Hindu-Arabic numeral system. The earliest evidence of the use of these numerals dates back to the 6th century CE.",
      "date": "0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction to the Islamic World",
      "text": "The numerals were introduced to the Islamic world through translations of Indian mathematical texts. This occurred during the 7th and 8th centuries CE.",
      "date": "0700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption in the Islamic Golden Age",
      "text": "During the Islamic Golden Age, mathematicians like Al-Khwarizmi and Al-Kindi further developed and disseminated the use of Arabic numerals. This took place in the 9th century CE.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spread to Europe",
      "text": "Arabic numerals were introduced to Europe through translations of Arabic mathematical texts, particularly by scholars in Al-Andalus and Italy, around the 10th century CE.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fibonacci's Liber Abaci",
      "text": "In 1202, the Italian mathematician Fibonacci published 'Liber Abaci', which popularized the use of Arabic numerals in Europe.",
      "date": "1202"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption in Europe",
      "text": "By the 15th century, Arabic numerals had become widely adopted across Europe, replacing Roman numerals in most applications.",
      "date": "1400"
    }
  ]
}

