      "headline": "Formation of the Axis of Resistance",
      "text": "The Axis of Resistance was formed as an informal coalition of Iranian-supported militias and political organizations across the Middle East. It unites actors committed to countering the influence of the United States and Israel in the region. The concept began to take shape in the early 2000s.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hezbollah's Role in the Axis",
      "text": "Hezbollah, a Lebanese Shia Islamist political party and militant group, became a key member of the Axis of Resistance. It has been supported by Iran since its inception in the 1980s and plays a significant role in the coalition.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Syrian Civil War Involvement",
      "text": "During the Syrian Civil War, the Axis of Resistance, particularly Iran and Hezbollah, provided substantial support to the Syrian government led by Bashar al-Assad. This involvement became prominent after the outbreak of the war in 2011.",
      "date": "2011"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Support for Houthi Rebels in Yemen",
      "text": "The Axis of Resistance extended its influence to Yemen by supporting the Houthi rebels in their conflict against the Yemeni government and the Saudi-led coalition. This support became more visible after the escalation of the Yemeni Civil War in 2015.",
      "date": "2015"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Involvement in Iraq",
      "text": "In Iraq, the Axis of Resistance includes various Shia militia groups, such as Kata'ib Hezbollah and Asa'ib Ahl al-Haq, which have been active in the region, especially after the rise of ISIS in 2014.",
      "date": "2014"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of the Donetsk People's Republic",
      "text": "On April 7, 2014, pro-Russian activists declared the establishment of the Donetsk People's Republic (DPR) following the occupation of the regional administration building in Donetsk, Ukraine.",
      "date": "2014-04-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Referendum on Self-Determination",
      "text": "On May 11, 2014, a controversial referendum was held in the Donetsk region, where separatists claimed a majority voted in favor of self-rule. The referendum was not recognized by Ukraine or the international community.",
      "date": "2014-05-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Declaration of Independence",
      "text": "On May 12, 2014, the DPR declared independence from Ukraine, following the unrecognized referendum.",
      "date": "2014-05-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Conflict with Ukrainian Forces",
      "text": "Throughout 2014, the DPR was involved in ongoing conflict with Ukrainian forces as part of the larger War in Donbas, which began in April 2014.",
      "date": "2014"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Minsk Agreements",
      "text": "In September 2014 and February 2015, the Minsk Protocol and Minsk II agreements were signed to establish a ceasefire and a framework for peace, although fighting continued intermittently.",
      "date": "2014-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of Constitution",
      "text": "On May 14, 2014, the DPR adopted its own constitution, further solidifying its claim to independence.",
      "date": "2014-05-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration with Russia",
      "text": "In 2022, following the Russian invasion of Ukraine, the DPR was recognized by Russia, and steps were taken towards integration with Russia, including the use of the Russian ruble and Russian legal frameworks.",
      "date": "2022"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Annexation by Russia",
      "text": "On September 30, 2022, Russia formally annexed the Donetsk People's Republic along with other occupied territories in Ukraine, following referendums that were widely condemned as illegitimate by the international community.",
      "date": "2022-09-30"
    }
  ]
}

