      "headline": "Introduction of the Civil Rights Act",
      "text": "The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was introduced in Congress by Representative Emanuel Celler and Senator Hubert Humphrey. The bill aimed to end segregation in public places and banned employment discrimination on the basis of race, color, religion, sex, or national origin.",
      "date": "1963-06-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "House of Representatives Passes the Bill",
      "text": "The House of Representatives passed the Civil Rights Act of 1964 after extensive debate and amendments. The bill faced significant opposition but was eventually passed with a vote of 290 to 130.",
      "date": "1964-02-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Senate Filibuster",
      "text": "A group of Southern Senators launched a filibuster to block the passage of the Civil Rights Act. The filibuster lasted for 60 working days, making it one of the longest in U.S. Senate history.",
      "date": "1964-03-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cloture Motion Ends Filibuster",
      "text": "The Senate voted for cloture, effectively ending the filibuster. This was the first time in history that the Senate invoked cloture on a civil rights bill, with a vote of 71 to 29.",
      "date": "1964-06-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Senate Passes the Civil Rights Act",
      "text": "Following the end of the filibuster, the Senate passed the Civil Rights Act of 1964 with a vote of 73 to 27.",
      "date": "1964-06-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Signing of the Civil Rights Act",
      "text": "President Lyndon B. Johnson signed the Civil Rights Act of 1964 into law at the White House. The signing ceremony was attended by prominent civil rights leaders, including Martin Luther King Jr.",
      "date": "1964-07-02"
    }
  ]
}

