      "headline": "Origins of Cantonese",
      "text": "Cantonese originated in the city of Guangzhou and its surrounding Pearl River Delta. It is a traditional prestige variety of Yue Chinese, a Sinitic language belonging to the Sino-Tibetan language family.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tang Dynasty Influence",
      "text": "During the Tang Dynasty (618–907), Guangzhou became a major trading port, which facilitated the spread and development of Cantonese as a distinct dialect.",
      "date": "0618"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ming Dynasty Standardization",
      "text": "In the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644), Cantonese began to be standardized as the official language of the region, further distinguishing it from other Chinese dialects.",
      "date": "1368"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Qing Dynasty and Cantonese Opera",
      "text": "During the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912), Cantonese opera emerged as a popular cultural form, contributing to the spread and cultural significance of the Cantonese language.",
      "date": "1644"
    },
    {
      "headline": "19th Century Migration",
      "text": "In the 19th century, significant migration from the Guangdong region to Southeast Asia and North America spread Cantonese globally, establishing it as a major Chinese dialect overseas.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "20th Century Media and Film",
      "text": "In the 20th century, Cantonese became the dominant language of Hong Kong cinema and media, further solidifying its cultural influence.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-1997 Hong Kong",
      "text": "After the 1997 handover of Hong Kong to China, Cantonese remained the primary language of the region, despite increasing influence from Mandarin.",
      "date": "1997"
    }
  ]
}

{
