      "headline": "Creation of Salvator Mundi",
      "text": "The painting Salvator Mundi is believed to have been created by Leonardo da Vinci around 1499 to 1510. It depicts Christ as Salvator Mundi (Savior of the World), dressed in Renaissance clothing, giving a benediction with his right hand raised and two fingers extended, while holding a transparent, non-refracting crystal orb in his left hand.",
      "date": "1499-1510"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rediscovery and Restoration",
      "text": "Salvator Mundi was rediscovered in 2005. It underwent extensive restoration to remove overpainting and reveal the original work. The restoration process was crucial in re-evaluating the painting's attribution to Leonardo da Vinci.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exhibition at the National Gallery, London",
      "text": "The restored Salvator Mundi was included in an exhibition of Leonardo da Vinci's work at the National Gallery in London from 2011 to 2012. This exhibition played a significant role in the painting's recognition and attribution discussions.",
      "date": "2011-2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sale at Christie's",
      "text": "On November 15, 2017, Salvator Mundi was sold at Christie's auction house in New York for $450.3 million, setting a new record for the most expensive painting ever sold at auction. The sale further fueled debates over its attribution to Leonardo da Vinci.",
      "date": "2017-11-15"
    }
  ]
}

