      "headline": "Albert Einstein publishes the theory of special relativity",
      "text": "In 1905, Albert Einstein published his theory of special relativity, which introduced the concept of mass–energy equivalence. This theory revolutionized the understanding of physics by establishing that mass and energy are interchangeable.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Einstein's famous equation E=mc² is introduced",
      "text": "In 1905, as part of his special relativity paper, Einstein introduced the equation E=mc², which expresses the mass–energy equivalence principle. This equation states that the energy (E) of a system is equal to its mass (m) multiplied by the speed of light squared (c²).",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Experimental confirmation of mass–energy equivalence",
      "text": "In the 1930s, experiments in nuclear physics began to confirm the mass–energy equivalence principle. The conversion of mass into energy was observed in nuclear reactions, providing empirical evidence for Einstein's equation.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of nuclear energy and weapons",
      "text": "During the 1940s, the principle of mass–energy equivalence was applied in the development of nuclear energy and nuclear weapons. The release of energy from nuclear reactions demonstrated the practical implications of E=mc².",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mass–energy equivalence in particle physics",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, mass–energy equivalence became a fundamental concept in particle physics, helping to explain the behavior of particles in high-energy environments and the creation of particles in particle accelerators.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

