    {
      "headline": "Beginning of the Feud",
      "text": "The Hatfield–McCoy feud is believed to have started in 1863, during the American Civil War, when Asa Harmon McCoy, a Union soldier, was killed by a group of Confederate Home Guards called the 'Logan Wildcats,' which included members of the Hatfield family.",
      "date": "1863"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dispute over a Hog",
      "text": "In 1878, a dispute over the ownership of a hog escalated tensions between the Hatfields and McCoys. Floyd Hatfield, a cousin of Devil Anse Hatfield, was accused by Randolph McCoy of stealing his hog. The case went to trial, and Floyd Hatfield was acquitted, further fueling the feud.",
      "date": "1878"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Election Day Fight",
      "text": "On August 7, 1882, a fight broke out between members of the two families during an election day event. Ellison Hatfield was stabbed 26 times and shot in the back by three McCoy brothers, Tolbert, Pharmer, and Bud. This incident led to further violence.",
      "date": "1882-08-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hatfield Retaliation",
      "text": "On August 9, 1882, after Ellison Hatfield died from his injuries, the Hatfields captured the three McCoy brothers responsible for his death and executed them by tying them to pawpaw bushes and shooting them.",
      "date": "1882-08-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New Year's Night Massacre",
      "text": "On January 1, 1888, the Hatfields attacked the McCoy family cabin, killing two of Randolph McCoy's children and severely injuring his wife. This event is known as the New Year's Night Massacre.",
      "date": "1888-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Grapevine Creek",
      "text": "In January 1888, the Battle of Grapevine Creek occurred, where a posse of McCoys and law enforcement officers attempted to arrest members of the Hatfield family. The battle resulted in several deaths and injuries.",
      "date": "1888-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Feud",
      "text": "The feud officially ended in 1891 when the families agreed to a truce. The legal battles and violence had subsided, and both families moved on from the conflict.",
      "date": "1891"
    }
  ]
}

