      "headline": "Creation of RSS 0.90",
      "text": "RSS 0.90 was created by Netscape in March 1999 as a format for building portals of news headlines.",
      "date": "1999-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of RSS 0.91",
      "text": "In July 1999, RSS 0.91 was introduced, simplifying the format and adding support for additional metadata.",
      "date": "1999-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of RSS 1.0",
      "text": "RSS 1.0 was developed by the RSS-DEV Working Group and released in December 2000, incorporating RDF (Resource Description Framework) to allow for more complex data structures.",
      "date": "2000-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release of RSS 2.0",
      "text": "In September 2002, RSS 2.0 was released by Dave Winer, which added support for namespaces and allowed for extensions.",
      "date": "2002-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by Major News Outlets",
      "text": "Throughout the early 2000s, major news outlets began adopting RSS feeds to distribute their content, significantly increasing the format's popularity.",
      "date": "2000-2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Atom",
      "text": "In 2003, Atom was introduced as an alternative to RSS, addressing some of the limitations and ambiguities in the RSS format.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Integration into Browsers",
      "text": "By the mid-2000s, web browsers such as Firefox and Internet Explorer began integrating RSS feed readers directly into their software, making it easier for users to subscribe to feeds.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Popularity",
      "text": "In the 2010s, the popularity of RSS began to decline with the rise of social media platforms and other content aggregation methods.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

