      "headline": "Discovery of Botulinum Toxin",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, the bacterium Clostridium botulinum was identified as the cause of botulism, a serious illness caused by the toxin it produces.",
      "date": "1895"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isolation of Botulinum Toxin",
      "text": "In 1944, Dr. Edward Schantz and his colleagues successfully isolated botulinum toxin in crystalline form, paving the way for its study and potential uses.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Therapeutic Use",
      "text": "In 1977, Dr. Alan B. Scott used botulinum toxin type A to treat strabismus (crossed eyes) in monkeys, marking the first therapeutic application of the toxin.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Strabismus and Blepharospasm",
      "text": "In 1989, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved botulinum toxin type A for the treatment of strabismus and blepharospasm (uncontrollable blinking).",
      "date": "1989"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Cosmetic Use",
      "text": "In 2002, the FDA approved botulinum toxin type A, marketed as Botox, for cosmetic use to temporarily improve the appearance of frown lines between the eyebrows.",
      "date": "2002"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Approval for Chronic Migraine",
      "text": "In 2010, the FDA approved botulinum toxin type A for the prevention of chronic migraines in adults.",
      "date": "2010"
