    {
      "headline": "First Definition of the Mandelbrot Set",
      "text": "The Mandelbrot set is defined as the set of complex numbers c for which the function f_c(z) = z^2 + c does not diverge to infinity when iterated from z = 0. This definition was first formalized in the late 1970s.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Benoit Mandelbrot's Work",
      "text": "Benoit Mandelbrot, a French-American mathematician, studied and popularized the Mandelbrot set in the late 1970s and early 1980s. His work on fractals and the Mandelbrot set was crucial in bringing these concepts to a wider audience.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Fractal Geometry of Nature'",
      "text": "In 1982, Benoit Mandelbrot published 'The Fractal Geometry of Nature', which included discussions on the Mandelbrot set and its properties. This book helped to popularize fractals and the Mandelbrot set among both scientists and the general public.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Advancements in Computer Graphics",
      "text": "During the 1980s, advancements in computer graphics allowed for the detailed visualization of the Mandelbrot set, revealing its intricate and beautiful fractal structures. This contributed to its popularity as a subject of study and artistic inspiration.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
