      "headline": "Introduction of Manhwa",
      "text": "Manhwa, the general Korean term for comics and print cartoons, began to take shape in the early 20th century, influenced by Japanese manga and Western comics.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Japanese Occupation Influence",
      "text": "During the Japanese occupation of Korea (1910-1945), Japanese manga heavily influenced the development of manhwa.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Liberation Boom",
      "text": "After Korea's liberation in 1945, manhwa experienced a boom, with many artists creating works that reflected the social and political changes of the time.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Golden Age of Manhwa",
      "text": "The 1960s and 1970s are often referred to as the Golden Age of Manhwa, with a surge in popularity and the emergence of many notable artists and series.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Censorship and Decline",
      "text": "In the 1980s, strict censorship laws in South Korea led to a decline in the manhwa industry, as many works were banned or heavily edited.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Webtoons",
      "text": "In the early 2000s, the advent of the internet led to the rise of webtoons, digital comics that revitalized the manhwa industry and expanded its global reach.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Popularity",
      "text": "By the 2010s, manhwa and webtoons gained significant international popularity, leading to adaptations into movies, dramas, and television shows.",
