      "headline": "Collision of SS Mont-Blanc and SS Imo",
      "text": "On the morning of 6 December 1917, the French cargo ship SS Mont-Blanc collided with the Norwegian vessel SS Imo in the harbour of Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada.",
      "date": "1917-12-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Explosion of SS Mont-Blanc",
      "text": "After the collision, SS Mont-Blanc, laden with high explosives, caught fire and exploded, devastating the Richmond district of Halifax. The explosion occurred on 6 December 1917.",
      "date": "1917-12-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Casualties and Damage",
      "text": "The explosion killed at least 1,782 people, largely in Halifax and Dartmouth, by the blast, debris, fires, or collapsed buildings. An estimated 9,000 others were injured. The explosion was the largest human-made explosion at the time, releasing the equivalent energy of roughly 2.9 kilotons of TNT.",
      "date": "1917-12-06"
    }
  ]
}

