      "headline": "Treaty of 1868",
      "text": "On June 1, 1868, the Treaty of Bosque Redondo was signed, allowing the Navajo people to return to their homeland after being forcibly relocated to Bosque Redondo in New Mexico.",
      "date": "1868-06-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Navajo Reservation",
      "text": "In 1868, following the Treaty of Bosque Redondo, the Navajo Reservation was established, marking the beginning of the Navajo Nation's formal recognition.",
      "date": "1868"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Navajo Reservation",
      "text": "Between 1878 and 1934, the Navajo Reservation expanded significantly through a series of executive orders and congressional acts, increasing its size to over 27,000 square miles.",
      "date": "1934"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Navajo Tribal Council Formation",
      "text": "In 1923, the Navajo Tribal Council was formed to facilitate negotiations with oil companies, marking the beginning of formalized governance for the Navajo Nation.",
      "date": "1923"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Navajo Code Talkers",
      "text": "During World War II, from 1942 to 1945, the Navajo Code Talkers played a crucial role in the Pacific Theater by using the Navajo language to create an unbreakable code for secure military communications.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Navajo Nation Council Reorganization",
      "text": "In 1991, the Navajo Nation Council was reorganized to separate the executive, legislative, and judicial branches, enhancing the governance structure of the Navajo Nation.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Navajo Nation Gaming Enterprises",
      "text": "In 2004, the Navajo Nation Gaming Enterprises was established, leading to the opening of several casinos and contributing to the economic development of the Navajo Nation.",
      "date": "2004"
    }
  ]
}

