      "headline": "Founding of the Golden Raspberry Awards",
      "text": "The Golden Raspberry Awards, also known as the Razzies, were founded by UCLA film graduates and film industry veterans John J. B. Wilson and Mo Murphy. The awards were created to honor the worst cinematic failures. The first ceremony was held on March 31, 1981.",
      "date": "1981-03-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Golden Raspberry Awards Ceremony",
      "text": "The inaugural Golden Raspberry Awards ceremony took place on March 31, 1981, in John J. B. Wilson's living room in Hollywood. The event was attended by 36 people and was a parody of the Academy Awards.",
      "date": "1981-03-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Razzie Statuette",
      "text": "The Razzie statuette, awarded to winners, is a golf ball-sized raspberry atop a Super 8mm film reel atop a 35-millimeter film core with brown wood shelf paper glued and wrapped around it, sitting atop a jar lid spray-painted gold.",
      "date": "1981"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Public Razzie Ceremony",
      "text": "The first public Razzie ceremony was held in 1982, marking the beginning of the awards' public recognition and media coverage.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Razzie Awards Gain Popularity",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, the Razzie Awards gained popularity and media attention, becoming a well-known event in the film industry.",
      "date": "1980s-1990s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "25th Anniversary of the Razzies",
      "text": "In 2005, the Golden Raspberry Awards celebrated their 25th anniversary, marking a quarter-century of recognizing the worst in cinema.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Razzie Redeemer Award Introduced",
      "text": "In 2014, the Razzie Redeemer Award was introduced, recognizing past Razzie winners who have gone on to achieve success in their careers.",
      "date": "2014"
    }
  ]
}

