      "text": "In 1850, President Millard Fillmore signed an order reserving the island for military use, marking the beginning of its development as a military fortification.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of Fort Alcatraz",
      "text": "In 1853, construction began on Fort Alcatraz, which was completed in 1859. It was equipped with long-range cannons and served as a military defense site.",
      "date": "1853"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Military Prison Established",
      "text": "In 1868, Alcatraz was officially designated as a long-term military prison, housing Civil War prisoners and later, military prisoners.",
      "date": "1868"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Alcatraz Becomes a Federal Penitentiary",
      "text": "In 1934, the island was converted into a federal prison, known as Alcatraz Federal Penitentiary, designed to hold the most dangerous criminals.",
      "date": "1934"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Notorious Inmates Arrive",
      "text": "Throughout the 1930s and 1940s, Alcatraz housed infamous criminals such as Al Capone, George 'Machine Gun' Kelly, and Robert Stroud, the 'Birdman of Alcatraz'.",
      "date": "1930s-1940s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Battle of Alcatraz",
      "text": "In May 1946, a failed escape attempt led to a violent confrontation known as the Battle of Alcatraz, resulting in the deaths of three inmates and two guards.",
      "date": "1946-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Closure of Alcatraz Prison",
      "text": "On March 21, 1963, Alcatraz Federal Penitentiary was closed due to high operating costs and deteriorating facilities.",
      "date": "1963-03-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Occupation by Native American Activists",
      "text": "From November 20, 1969, to June 11, 1971, Native American activists occupied Alcatraz Island, demanding the land be returned to Native peoples.",
      "date": "1969-11-20"
    },
