      "text": "The first settlement at Knossos dates back to around 7000 BCE, during the Neolithic period.",
      "date": "-7000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of the Minoan Civilization",
      "text": "Around 2000 BCE, Knossos became a major center of the Minoan civilization, characterized by the construction of the first palace.",
      "date": "-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of the First Palace",
      "text": "The first palace at Knossos was constructed around 1900 BCE, marking the beginning of the Protopalatial period.",
      "date": "-1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Destruction and Rebuilding of the Palace",
      "text": "Around 1700 BCE, the palace was destroyed, possibly by an earthquake, and was subsequently rebuilt during the Neopalatial period.",
      "date": "-1700"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Height of Minoan Civilization",
      "text": "Knossos reached its peak during the Neopalatial period, around 1600-1450 BCE, with extensive trade networks and cultural influence.",
      "date": "-1600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Final Destruction of the Palace",
      "text": "The palace at Knossos was finally destroyed around 1375 BCE, marking the end of the Minoan civilization's dominance.",
      "date": "-1375"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mycenaean Occupation",
      "text": "After the destruction, the site was occupied by Mycenaeans, who used it until around 1200 BCE.",
      "date": "-1200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abandonment of Knossos",
      "text": "Knossos was eventually abandoned around 1100 BCE, following the decline of the Mycenaean civilization.",
      "date": "-1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rediscovery and Excavation",
      "text": "The site of Knossos was rediscovered in the late 19th century and extensively excavated by Sir Arthur Evans starting in 1900.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

