      "headline": "Discovery of Io",
      "text": "Io was discovered on January 8, 1610, by Galileo Galilei. It was one of the first four moons of Jupiter to be discovered, now known as the Galilean moons.",
      "date": "1610-01-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Io",
      "text": "Io was named after the mythological character Io, a priestess of Hera who became one of Zeus's lovers. The name was suggested by Simon Marius, who claimed to have discovered the moon independently around the same time as Galileo.",
      "date": "1614"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Voyager 1 Flyby",
      "text": "In March 1979, NASA's Voyager 1 spacecraft conducted a flyby of Io, providing the first detailed images of its surface and revealing its numerous active volcanoes.",
      "date": "1979-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Voyager 2 Flyby",
      "text": "Voyager 2 flew by Io in July 1979, capturing additional images and data that confirmed the moon's volcanic activity.",
      "date": "1979-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Galileo Spacecraft Observations",
      "text": "From 1995 to 2003, NASA's Galileo spacecraft conducted extensive observations of Io, greatly enhancing our understanding of its geology and volcanic activity.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New Horizons Flyby",
      "text": "In February 2007, the New Horizons spacecraft flew past Io, capturing images and data that provided further insights into its volcanic activity and surface composition.",
      "date": "2007-02"
