      "headline": "Early Roots of Unitarianism",
      "text": "The roots of Unitarianism can be traced back to early Christian theological debates in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, where some groups rejected the doctrine of the Trinity.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Michael Servetus' Execution",
      "text": "In 1553, Michael Servetus, a Spanish physician and theologian, was executed for heresy in Geneva. He rejected the doctrine of the Trinity and is considered a significant figure in the history of Unitarianism.",
      "date": "1553"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Polish Brethren",
      "text": "In 1565, the Polish Brethren, a Unitarian group, was established in Poland. They were influential in spreading Unitarian ideas in Eastern Europe.",
      "date": "1565"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Unitarian Church in Transylvania",
      "text": "In 1568, the Diet of Torda in Transylvania, under the influence of Ferenc Dávid, recognized the Unitarian Church, making it one of the first regions to officially accept Unitarianism.",
      "date": "1568"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Biddle's Influence in England",
      "text": "In the mid-17th century, John Biddle, often called the 'Father of English Unitarianism', published works challenging the doctrine of the Trinity, leading to the spread of Unitarian ideas in England.",
      "date": "1640"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the American Unitarian Association",
      "text": "In 1825, the American Unitarian Association was formed in Boston, Massachusetts, marking the formal organization of Unitarianism in the United States.",
      "date": "1825"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Unitarian Universalist Association Merger",
      "text": "In 1961, the Unitarian Church merged with the Universalist Church of America to form the Unitarian Universalist Association, reflecting a broader theological and social outlook.",
      "date": "1961"
    }
  ]
}

