      "headline": "Lynyrd Skynyrd Plane Crash",
      "text": "On October 20, 1977, a Convair CV-240 passenger aircraft carrying the rock band Lynyrd Skynyrd ran out of fuel and crashed in a wooded area near Gillsburg, Mississippi. The plane was en route from Greenville, South Carolina, to Baton Rouge, Louisiana.",
      "date": "1977-10-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fatalities and Injuries",
      "text": "The crash resulted in the deaths of band members Ronnie Van Zant, Steve Gaines, and Cassie Gaines, along with assistant road manager Dean Kilpatrick, pilot Walter McCreary, and co-pilot William Gray. Several other band members and crew were seriously injured.",
      "date": "1977-10-20"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation and Cause",
      "text": "The National Transportation Safety Board (NTSB) investigated the crash and determined that the probable cause was fuel exhaustion and total loss of power from both engines due to crew inattention to fuel supply.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Impact on Lynyrd Skynyrd",
      "text": "The crash had a profound impact on the band, leading to a temporary disbandment. Surviving members eventually reformed the band in 1987 with Ronnie Van Zant's younger brother, Johnny Van Zant, as the lead vocalist.",
      "date": "1987"
    }
  ]
}

